The rule review exposed a material domain mismatch, not just an evidence gap. The trusted prompt does not exclude Booleans or mixed numeric values, and both Python implementations return `[True]` for `common([True], [1])`. The supplied membership rules use structural `==K`, so fresh LLVM execution computes an empty intersection and ends with `AssertionError`/exit code 1 on that same valid input. This is the required concrete false-conclusion witness; I’m recording it against the exact rules before final accounting.
